      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1853, property developer Thomas Lord officially laid out Egleston Square, naming it after the nearby Egleston Hill.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.jphs.org/locales/2005/9/30/egleston-square-by-richard-heath.html",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The square’s streets were originally part of a 4000‑acre grant to Roxbury in 1638, marking the town’s western boundary with Dedham.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.jphs.org/locales/2005/9/30/egleston-square-by-richard-heath.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Washington Street, which runs through Egleston Square, was named in 1874 after the turnpike that connected Roxbury to Dedham and Norwood.",
